{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואדני זקן", :translit "v'adoni zakein", :gloss "and my master is old"} {:he "טוחן ולא פולט", :translit "tochen v'lo poleit", :gloss "grinding but not releasing"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "ואדני זקן", :b "טוחן ולא פולט"}], :frame {:text "Sarah laughs at the promise of a son, saying to herself, 'Now that I am withered, shall I have pleasure — and my master is old?' (Genesis 18:12). The phrase 'and my master is old' is read as an allusion to Abraham's advanced age and diminished vigor. Rabbinic tradition discusses Sarah's laughter as reflecting disbelief at renewed intimacy in old age (Genesis Rabbah 48:17). The Ba'al HaTurim employs gematria to encode a physiological reading: the letters of 'ואדני זקן' equal those of 'טוחן ולא פולט,' suggesting Abraham's body grinds yet cannot release seed.", :sources ["Genesis 18:12" "Genesis Rabbah 48:17"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "ואדני זקן", :a-value 228, :b "טוחן ולא פולט", :b-value 235, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:06:33.458191065Z", :model "glm-5.2"}